{"id":"f9f84b8c4c5f49fd35381ce07f7f6a1c8ed977cccd1db09f99221ecde95a5695","lamport":1,"author":"carlo.cifarelli@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-10T13:01:37.187878Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"description":"Linked git worktrees got an **isolated** ticket store. `TicketStore::discover()`\nopened `git-meta.sqlite` under the per-worktree git-dir (what `gix::discover`\nyields inside a linked worktree) instead of the shared common git-dir.\n\nConsequences:\n- `ti list` in a worktree showed none of the main checkout's tickets.\n- A ticket created in a worktree (synced to origin) **vanished** once the\n  worktree was removed — the op lived in `refs/meta/*` but was never in the\n  main checkout's store.\n\n## Fix\n\nResolve the store under the repo's **common** git-dir so every worktree of a\nrepo shares one `git-meta.sqlite`.\n\nAlso add `ti reindex` to rebuild a store from `refs/meta/*` when ops exist in\nthe refs but are missing from local sqlite (the stranded-ticket case above, or\na lost store file). It drops the local serialization ref so git-meta cannot\nshort-circuit as up-to-date, then materializes the remote tracking ref back\ninto the store. Non-destructive; guarded to refuse unless a fetched meta ref\nexists, so the only serialized copy is never dropped when it cannot be rebuilt.\n\n## Tests\n\nCLI integration regressions: a linked worktree sees the main checkout's\ntickets; a worktree-born ticket is visible in the main checkout after the\nworktree is removed; `ti reindex` rebuilds the store after the sqlite file is\ndeleted.\n\n## Docs\n\n`ti reindex` documented in the `ti agent` guide, `docs/agents.md`, README\nmaintenance table, and CLI help.\n\nShipped in v0.4.0 (commit 4757bdd). Filed retroactively — work predates this\nticket."}}}